        <span class="badge">A-Z</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Darts Glossary and Slang (A-Z)</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">Professional darts possesses one of the richest, most colorful vocabularies in world sport. Born in centuries-old British public houses and elevated onto global television stages by iconic commentators like Sid Waddell and John Gwynne, darts terminology combines razor-sharp mathematical precision with playful pub folklore.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container content-layout">
    <main class="main-column">
      <VerificationBadge verifiedDate="October 2026" topic="Darts Terminology" />

      <section class="content-card">

<p>Whether you are watching the PDC World Darts Championship at Alexandra Palace, calculating your outshot at a local league match, or learning why hitting the wire is called a "wire bounce," this definitive directory explains every vital term, rule, and piece of slang from A to Z.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Quick Navigation: Jump to Letter</h2>
<p><a href="#letter-a">A</a> | <a href="#letter-b">B</a> | <a href="#letter-c">C</a> | <a href="#letter-d">D</a> | <a href="#letter-e">E</a> | <a href="#letter-f">F</a> | <a href="#letter-g">G</a> | <a href="#letter-h">H</a> | <a href="#letter-i">I</a> | <a href="#letter-j">J</a> | <a href="#letter-k">K</a> | <a href="#letter-l">L</a> | <a href="#letter-m">M</a> | <a href="#letter-n">N</a> | <a href="#letter-o">O</a> | <a href="#letter-p">P</a> | <a href="#letter-q">Q</a> | <a href="#letter-r">R</a> | <a href="#letter-s">S</a> | <a href="#letter-t">T</a> | <a href="#letter-u">U</a> | <a href="#letter-v">V</a> | <a href="#letter-w">W</a> | <a href="#letter-x">X</a> | <a href="#letter-y">Y</a> | <a href="#letter-z">Z</a></p>
      </section>
      <AdSlot position="in-article" />
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Complete A–Z Darts Term Index</h2>
<p>Below is the master index of over 85 professional and grassroots darts terms. Every entry provides a concise, plain-English definition, with links to dedicated guides for in-depth rules and strategy.</p>
<p><code>+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|                   STANDARD DARTBOARD ANATOMY                    |
|                                                                 |
|                     [ DOUBLE RING (Outer) ]                     |
|                               |                                 |
|                     [ OUTER SINGLE BED ]                        |
|                               |                                 |
|                     [ TREBLE RING (Inner) ]                     |
|                               |                                 |
|                     [ INNER SINGLE BED ]                        |
|                               |                                 |
|               [ 25: SINGLE BULL / OUTER BULL ]                  |
|                               |                                 |
|                   [ 50: BULLSEYE / DOUBLE ]                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+</code></p>
<h3>Letter A</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-a"></a><strong>Aiming:</strong> The visual alignment of the eye, dart tip, and target prior to initiating the backward draw and forward acceleration of the throwing arm. See our <a href="/darts/how-to-throw-darts/">How to Throw Darts Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Ally Pally:</strong> The affectionate colloquial name for Alexandra Palace in North London, the iconic home venue of the PDC World Darts Championship since December 2007.</li>
<li><strong>Annie's Room:</strong> Traditional British pub slang for <strong>Double 1</strong>, the lowest scoring double on the board (synonymous with <em>The Madhouse</em>).</li>
<li><strong>Around the Clock:</strong> A classic foundational practice drill where a player attempts to hit every number from 1 to 20 in sequential order, often finishing on the Bullseye. Read our <a href="/darts/practice-routines/">Practice Routines Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Arrows:</strong> Universal popular slang term for darts.</li>
<li><strong>Averages (3-Dart):</strong> The universally recognized statistical benchmark measuring the average points scored per visit of three darts thrown throughout a leg, match, or tournament. Learn the formula in our <a href="/darts/average-explained/">3-Dart Average Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter B</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-b"></a><strong>Baby Fish:</strong> Slang for a checkout of <strong>130</strong>, achieved via Triple 20, Triple 18, and Double 8 (or Bullseye variations), referencing a junior sibling to the 170 "Big Fish".</li>
<li><strong>Bag of Nails:</strong> Traditional pub term for scoring <strong>26 points</strong> in a single visit, typically by hitting a Single 20, Single 1, and Single 5 when aiming for the treble 20 (also called <em>Breakfast</em> or <em>Bed &amp; Breakfast</em>).</li>
<li><strong>Barn Dart:</strong> The third and final dart of a three-dart visit, particularly when it successfully salvages an otherwise poor turn or hits the required target under extreme pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Barney Army:</strong> The legendary global chant and fan contingent that loyally followed five-time World Champion Raymond van Barneveld throughout his illustrious career. See our <a href="/fan-culture/chants-songs/">Darts Chants &amp; Fan Songs Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Barrel:</strong> The central metal body of the dart held by the player, typically manufactured from dense tungsten nickel alloys to allow slim profiles for tighter grouping.</li>
<li><strong>Bed:</strong> Any single designated scoring section between the metal dividing wires (spiders) on a dartboard.</li>
<li><strong>Bed and Breakfast:</strong> Popular British slang for a 3-dart score of <strong>26</strong> (traditionally 2 shillings and 6 pence, the historical price of a modest bed-and-breakfast stay).</li>
<li><strong>Big Fish:</strong> The affectionate nickname for a <strong>170 checkout</strong>—the absolute maximum legal finish in standard 501 darts (Treble 20, Treble 20, Bullseye). Explore why in our <a href="/darts/why-is-170-the-highest-checkout/">170 Checkout Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Blind 180:</strong> A maximum score of 180 thrown so quickly or with the first two barrels obscuring the bed that the player cannot clearly see the target for their third dart.</li>
<li><strong>Board Height:</strong> The official regulatory vertical distance from the center of the bullseye to the playing floor, strictly set at <strong>5 feet 8 inches (1.73 meters)</strong>. See our <a href="/darts/board-height-and-distance/">Board Height &amp; Distance Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Bogey Numbers:</strong> Seven mathematical scores below 170 (<strong>169, 168, 166, 165, 163, 162, and 159</strong>) that are mathematically impossible to check out in a single three-dart visit under standard double-out rules. Master them in our <a href="/darts/bogey-numbers-in-darts/">Bogey Numbers Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Bounce-Out:</strong> A dart that strikes a wire, another dart, or the sisal fibers and falls to the floor before being recorded; under official DRA rules, bounce-outs score zero points.</li>
<li><strong>Break of Throw:</strong> Winning a leg in which the opponent had the first throw advantage, equivalent to a break of serve in tennis.</li>
<li><strong>Bristle Board:</strong> A high-grade tournament dartboard constructed from tightly packed sisal fibers (originating from Madagascar or East Africa) that self-heal when darts are extracted.</li>
<li><strong>Bull-Off (Corking):</strong> The pre-match ritual where each player throws a single dart at the Bullseye; the player whose dart lands closest earns the critical advantage of throwing first in the opening leg.</li>
<li><strong>Bullseye (Inner Bull):</strong> The red circular zone at the exact geometric center of the dartboard, measuring 12.7mm across, worth <strong>50 points</strong>, and legally counting as a double for checking out.</li>
<li><strong>Bust Rule:</strong> The rule stating that if a player scores more points than their remaining score, leaves a remainder of exactly 1, or reduces their score to zero without finishing on a valid double, their entire three-dart visit is voided, and their score reverts to what it was at the start of that turn. Read our <a href="/darts/bust-rule-in-darts/">Bust Rule Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter C</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-c"></a><strong>Caller:</strong> The licensed tournament referee (such as Kirk Bevins, George Noble, or Russ Bray) stationed beside the board who calls out scores to the arena crowd and broadcast audience.</li>
<li><strong>Chalker (Marker):</strong> The official or fellow player responsible for marking scores, calculating remaining totals, and updating the chalkboard or electronic scoring system during matches.</li>
<li><strong>Champagne Breakfast:</strong> Whimsical pub slang for hitting three Triple 1s in a single visit, yielding an unimpressive total of <strong>9 points</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Checkout:</strong> The final scoring shot (or combination of shots) that reduces a player's remaining score from an outshot value to exactly zero, concluding on a double or bullseye to win the leg. Reference our <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">PDC Checkout Chart</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Checkout Percentage:</strong> A vital modern performance metric calculating the ratio of successful checkout doubles hit relative to total double attempts registered in a match.</li>
<li><strong>Choke:</strong> Missing crucial match darts or doubles due to acute psychological tension or nerves on the oche.</li>
<li><strong>Circuit:</strong> The professional schedule of tournaments, comprising floor events, European Tour stages, and televised majors organized by the PDC.</li>
<li><strong>Clamping:</strong> Squeezing the fingers together tightly during the draw and forward motion, which restricts natural release and creates erratic horizontal flight path drift.</li>
<li><strong>Closing:</strong> In the game of American/Cricket darts, hitting a designated number three times (via three singles, a single and a double, or a treble) to make it eligible for point-scoring. Learn the rules in our <a href="/darts/how-to-play-cricket/">How to Play Cricket Darts Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Cork:</strong> An alternative, traditional American term for the Bullseye, derived from the historical practice of using keg cork bungs as targets in taverns.</li>
<li><strong>Cricket:</strong> The widely popular alternative darts game focused on closing numbers 15 through 20 and the Bullseye before scoring points against an opponent. See our <a href="/darts/how-to-play-cricket/">Cricket Rules &amp; Strategy Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter D</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-d"></a><strong>Dartitis:</strong> A severe, debilitating psychological condition similar to the "yips" in golf, where a player experiences an involuntary mental block or physical freeze that prevents them from releasing the dart smoothly.</li>
<li><strong>Dead Eye:</strong> Achieving three consecutive Bullseyes (inner 50) in a single three-dart visit, totaling 150 points.</li>
<li><strong>Decider:</strong> The final leg of a match or set when the score is level (e.g., 5–5 in a best-of-11 match), played as a sudden-death contest.</li>
<li><strong>Deflection:</strong> When an incoming dart collides with a dart already embedded in the sisal board, altering its trajectory into another bed, into the wire, or onto the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Double:</strong> The outer narrow ring on the dartboard, measuring 8mm wide, which doubles the numerical point value of the segment (e.g., Double 20 = 40 points).</li>
<li><strong>Double Bull:</strong> Another common name for the 50-point central Bullseye, distinguishing it from the 25-point single bull.</li>
<li><strong>Double-In Double-Out (DIDO):</strong> A demanding match format where a player must hit a double to begin scoring in a leg, as well as finishing on a double to win it (famously used at the PDC World Grand Prix). See our <a href="/darts/double-in-double-out/">Double-In Double-Out Explainer</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Double Trouble:</strong> A match state where one or both players repeatedly miss attempts at a finishing double across multiple consecutive visits, often causing averages to plummet.</li>
<li><strong>Downstairs:</strong> Commentary slang referring to the bottom half of the dartboard, most notably the <strong>Treble 19</strong> segment (worth 57 points), frequently visited when Treble 20 is blocked.</li>
<li><strong>DRA:</strong> The <strong>Darts Regulation Authority</strong>, the independent governing body responsible for professional darts rules, disciplinary procedures, player conduct, and equipment standards.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter E</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-e"></a><strong>Elbow Alignment:</strong> A core tenet of throwing biomechanics where the throwing elbow remains horizontally and vertically aligned with the target to ensure an unswerving pendulum arm action.</li>
<li><strong>Exhibition:</strong> A non-ranking promotional or entertainment darts showcase where professionals interact with audiences, perform trick shots, and compete in lighthearted formats.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter F</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-f"></a><strong>First-9 Average:</strong> The statistical scoring average calculated solely across a player's first nine darts (three visits) of each leg, providing a pristine measurement of raw power scoring before doubles distortion.</li>
<li><strong>Fish &amp; Chips:</strong> Humorous UK slang for scoring <strong>33 points</strong> (Single 20, Single 12, Single 1).</li>
<li><strong>Flight:</strong> The aerodynamic fin fitted into the rear of the shaft that stabilizes the dart's trajectory in mid-air and controls its entry angle into the board.</li>
<li><strong>Floor Tournament:</strong> Non-televised ranking events (such as PDC Players Championship floor days) played simultaneously across dozens of partition boards with minimal spectators.</li>
<li><strong>Follow-Through:</strong> The natural physical continuation of the throwing arm extending fully toward the target after the dart has left the fingers, essential for consistent release.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter G</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-g"></a><strong>Game On:</strong> The traditional call uttered by the referee or Master of Ceremonies to signal the start of a contest or set.</li>
<li><strong>Game Shot:</strong> The formal announcement delivered by the referee when a competitor successfully lands their final required double to win a leg, set, or match.</li>
<li><strong>Grouping:</strong> The physical cluster of three darts landing in close proximity on the board, demonstrating mechanical consistency in release and aim.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter H</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-h"></a><strong>Half a Crown:</strong> Traditional slang for scoring <strong>26 points</strong> (two shillings and sixpence in pre-decimal British coinage).</li>
<li><strong>Hat-Trick:</strong> Landing all three darts in the central Bullseye (50 points each) during a single visit, scoring 150 points.</li>
<li><strong>Hold of Throw:</strong> Winning a leg in which you threw first, successfully defending the mathematical advantage inherent to opening the scoring.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter I</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-i"></a><strong>Inner Bull:</strong> The central 50-point red disc (Bullseye).</li>
<li><strong>Island:</strong> The circular playing surface of the dartboard containing scoring segments, bounded by the non-scoring outer black ring.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter K</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-k"></a><strong>Key Checkouts:</strong> Pivotal two-dart and three-dart combination finishes (such as 121, 81, or 64) that offer flexible routes to a double while leaving clean backup targets if a treble is missed. Use our <a href="/tools/checkout-calculator/">Checkout Calculator Tool</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter L</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-l"></a><strong>Leg:</strong> A single game of 501. The basic building block of professional matchplay; the first player to reduce 501 to zero on a double wins the leg.</li>
<li><strong>Legs vs Sets:</strong> The two primary matchplay scoring structures. In "legs only" matches, the first to a target number of legs wins (e.g., first to 10). In "set play", players must win the best of 5 legs to secure a set, and the first to a target number of sets wins the match. Compare them in our <a href="/darts/legs-vs-sets/">Legs vs Sets Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter M</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-m"></a><strong>Madhouse:</strong> Universal darts slang for <strong>Double 1</strong> (worth 2 points). It is notoriously difficult to escape because any miss inside busts the score to zero, leaving players trapped in mental torment.</li>
<li><strong>Marker (Dart):</strong> A dart deliberately placed slightly above or below a target wire to act as a physical or visual guide for subsequent darts in the same visit.</li>
<li><strong>Master of Ceremonies (MC):</strong> The stage presenter (such as John McDonald) who introduces competitors, orchestrates walk-ons, and conducts on-stage post-match formalities. Learn about iconic stage voices in our <a href="/watch/commentators-presenters/">Commentators &amp; Presenters Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Maximum:</strong> A score of <strong>180 points</strong> achieved by landing all three darts cleanly into the Treble 20 bed in a single turn. Read our complete <a href="/darts/what-is-a-180-in-darts/">180 Maximum Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Mugging:</strong> A casual pub practice rule where the loser of the preceding leg must pay for drinks or mark the chalkboard for the next match.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter N</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-n"></a><strong>Nine-Dart Finish (Nine-Darter):</strong> The perfect game in 501 darts, requiring a competitor to check out 501 points using the theoretical minimum of nine consecutive darts. Explore every televised nine-darter in our <a href="/darts/what-is-a-9-dart-finish/">Nine-Dart Finish Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Nine-Dart Romance:</strong> A phrase coined by legendary commentator Sid Waddell to describe the dramatic, heart-pounding tension as a player steps up to attempt the final double of a nine-darter.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter O</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-o"></a><strong>Oche (The Throw Line):</strong> The raised line or toe-board behind which a player must stand when delivering their darts. The official distance from the face of the board to the rear of the raised oche is <strong>7 feet 9¼ inches (2.37 meters)</strong>. See our <a href="/darts/board-height-and-distance/">Board Height &amp; Distance Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Order of Merit:</strong> The official ranking table operated by the PDC, calculating player rankings based on prize money earned over a rolling two-year period in official ranking tournaments.</li>
<li><strong>Outer Bull:</strong> The green concentric ring surrounding the Bullseye, measuring 31.8mm across, worth <strong>25 points</strong>, counting as a single for scoring purposes.</li>
<li><strong>Outshot:</strong> Another term for a checkout; the successful reduction of a score to zero on a double.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter P</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-p"></a><strong>Pencil Grip:</strong> A throwing grip where the player holds the barrel between thumb and forefinger exactly like a writing instrument.</li>
<li><strong>Pie:</strong> Colloquial American term for any single triangular segment on the dartboard.</li>
<li><strong>Points:</strong> The steel or soft tips fitted into the front of the barrel that penetrate the board surface. Modern systems (like Target Swiss Points) allow rapid tip replacements with patented threaded tools.</li>
<li><strong>Pressure Double:</strong> A high-leverage checkout attempt taken when the opponent is sitting on an outshot, meaning a miss will almost certainly result in losing the leg.</li>
<li><strong>ProTour:</strong> The umbrella name for non-televised PDC ranking events, encompassing Players Championship floor tournaments and European Tour qualifiers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter Q</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-q"></a><strong>Q-School (Qualifying School):</strong> The annual PDC qualifying tournaments held each January in the UK and Germany, where hundreds of aspiring amateurs and tour veterans compete to earn two-year professional PDC Tour Cards.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter R</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-r"></a><strong>Right Side:</strong> Aiming at the numbers located on the right hemisphere of the board (e.g., 1, 18, 4, 13, 6, 10, 15, 2) to maneuver around blocked treble beds.</li>
<li><strong>Robin Hood:</strong> The extraordinary circumstance where a thrown dart embeds its steel point directly into the nylon shaft or plastic flight of a dart already in the board. Under DRA rules, a Robin Hood scores <strong>zero points</strong> because the point does not touch the board face. Read our <a href="/darts/robin-hood-in-darts/">Robin Hood In Darts Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Round:</strong> A single visit consisting of three darts thrown by one player.</li>
<li><strong>Round the Clock:</strong> Another term for the sequential 1-through-20 practice routine.</li>
<li><strong>Route:</strong> The tactical sequence of target segments chosen by a player to reach a preferred finishing double (e.g., 60-17-Bull for 127).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter S</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-s"></a><strong>Scorer:</strong> The computerized software application or tablet console operated by PDC markers to tabulate throws and broadcast real-time scoring data.</li>
<li><strong>Scoreboard:</strong> The physical chalk board, whiteboard, or digital LED display showing scores and remaining totals.</li>
<li><strong>Set:</strong> A structured unit of matchplay, typically contested as the best of five legs (first player to win 3 legs wins the set). Common at the World Darts Championship and World Grand Prix.</li>
<li><strong>Setup Shot:</strong> A tactical visit made when a player cannot check out in their current turn, aimed at leaving a specific, comfortable double or single for their subsequent visit (e.g., scoring 60 from 92 to leave Double 16).</li>
<li><strong>Shaft (Stem):</strong> The piece screwed into the back of the barrel that holds the flight, available in varying lengths (short, intermediate, medium) and materials (nylon, polycarbonate, titanium).</li>
<li><strong>Shanghai:</strong> A famous finish achieved by hitting the Single, Double, and Treble of the same numerical segment in a single three-dart turn (e.g., Shanghai 20 = Single 20, Double 20, Treble 20 for a 120 checkout). Discover how it works in our <a href="/darts/shanghai-finish-in-darts/">Shanghai Finish Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Sid Waddell Trophy:</strong> The prestigious championship trophy awarded annually to the winner of the PDC World Darts Championship, named in honor of the beloved Sky Sports commentator.</li>
<li><strong>Single:</strong> The large primary beds of a segment, counting as the face value of the number (1 through 20).</li>
<li><strong>Single Bull:</strong> The 25-point outer green ring surrounding the red Bullseye.</li>
<li><strong>Sisal:</strong> The natural botanical plant fiber derived from agave leaves, tightly bundled under intense hydraulic pressure to form the self-healing surface of modern bristle dartboards.</li>
<li><strong>Spider:</strong> The thin metal wire webbing that divides the numerical segments and rings on a dartboard. Modern professional boards use ultra-thin knife-edge blade wire to eliminate bounce-outs.</li>
<li><strong>Stem:</strong> Synonymous with <em>Shaft</em>; the rear component that connects the barrel to the flight.</li>
<li><strong>Straight-In:</strong> A match where scoring begins immediately with any dart, without requiring a starting double (standard 501 format).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter T</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-t"></a><strong>Table (Checkout Table):</strong> The mathematical matrix showing optimal combination paths for every checkout value between 2 and 170. Check our complete <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">Checkout Chart Table</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Three in a Bed:</strong> Landing all three darts of a single turn in the exact same scoring segment (e.g., three single 20s or three double 16s).</li>
<li><strong>Toe Line:</strong> Another term for the oche throw line.</li>
<li><strong>Ton:</strong> Traditional darts terminology for a score of exactly <strong>100 points</strong> in a single visit (e.g., Single 20 + Single 20 + Triple 20).</li>
<li><strong>Ton-Eighty (180):</strong> The maximum three-dart score achievable in darts, passionately announced by referees as <em>"ONE HUNDRED AND EIGHTY!"</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Ton-Forty (140):</strong> Scoring 140 points in a turn, typically two Triple 20s and a Single 20.</li>
<li><strong>Tops:</strong> Universal darts slang for <strong>Double 20</strong> (worth 40 points), named because it occupies the highest geometric double bed at the top edge of the board.</li>
<li><strong>Tour Card:</strong> The professional license issued to the elite 128 players entitled to compete on the full PDC ProTour circuit.</li>
<li><strong>Treble (Triple):</strong> The narrow inner ring on the board, measuring 8mm across, which triples the numerical point value of the segment (e.g., Treble 20 = 60 points).</li>
<li><strong>Tungsten:</strong> A dense, heavy transition metal alloyed with nickel and iron to manufacture professional dart barrels, allowing maximum weight in minimum barrel circumference.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter U</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-u"></a><strong>Unfinished:</strong> A player who has not yet concluded their leg or checked out their remaining score.</li>
<li><strong>Upstairs:</strong> Popular broadcast commentary slang referring to the upper hemisphere of the board, primarily targeting the <strong>Treble 20</strong> bed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter V</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-v"></a><strong>Visit:</strong> A single player's turn at the oche, consisting of throwing up to three darts before stepping aside for their opponent.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter W</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-w"></a><strong>Walk-On:</strong> The theatrical, music-fueled stage entrance made by professional players as they walk through the arena to the stage. Browse the full playlist in our <a href="/players/walk-on-songs/">PDC Walk-On Songs Directory</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Wiring (Wire):</strong> When a dart hits the metallic dividing spider rather than embedding into the sisal bed, often deflecting away or landing narrowly outside the target zone.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter Y</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-y"></a><strong>Yips:</strong> Another term for the anxiety-induced involuntary throwing stall known in darts as <em>dartitis</em>.</li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>The Four Vital Terminology Clusters</h2>
<p>To understand how professional players, commentators, and officials use these terms during tournament broadcasts, it helps to group them into four functional categories:</p>
<p><code>+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                  THE FOUR VOCABULARY PILLARS                |
|                                                             |
|   1. SCORING TERMS           2. FINISHING &amp; CHECKOUTS       |
|   - Maximum (180)            - The Big Fish (170)           |
|   - Ton / Ton-40             - Shanghai (120)               |
|   - Three in a Bed           - Bogey Numbers (169, 168...)  |
|   - Downstairs (T19)         - Tops / Madhouse              |
|                                                             |
|   3. MATCH &amp; RULES           4. CROWD &amp; PUB SLANG           |
|   - Bust Rule                - Robin Hood                   |
|   - Legs vs Sets             - Bed &amp; Breakfast (26)         |
|   - Double-In Double-Out     - Dartitis                     |
|   - Oche (7ft 9.25in)        - Chalker / Marker             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+</code></p>
<h3>1. Scoring Terms Explained</h3>
<p>In competitive 501, scoring is all about efficiency. While casual players often aim for the bullseye, professionals focus relentlessly on the <strong>Treble 20</strong> bed—worth 60 points per dart.
* <strong>The 180 (Maximum):</strong> The holy grail of a scoring turn. Placing three 8mm-wide tungsten barrels into an 8mm-tall slot requires sub-millimeter precision. Learn about historical maximum records in our <a href="/players/180-records/">180 Records Guide</a>.
* <strong>Downstairs Switching:</strong> When a player's first two darts block the Treble 20 bed, top professionals frequently switch "downstairs" to <strong>Treble 19</strong> (57 points) or <strong>Treble 18</strong> (54 points) to maintain high scoring cadence without risking flight deflections.</p>
<h3>2. Checkout &amp; Finish Terms Explained</h3>
<p>Finishing requires calculating mathematical combinations under extreme mental pressure:
* <strong>The 170 ("The Big Fish"):</strong> The highest possible outshot on a dartboard. It requires hitting two Treble 20s followed by the central 50-point Bullseye. Learn the full mathematical breakdown in our <a href="/darts/why-is-170-the-highest-checkout/">Why 170 is the Highest Checkout Guide</a>.
* <strong>Bogey Numbers:</strong> Any score between 159 and 169 with no three-dart solution. If a player is on 165 or 169, they cannot finish the leg regardless of where their darts land. A smart player uses their turn to set up an easy outshot for the next visit. Master them in our <a href="/darts/bogey-numbers-in-darts/">Bogey Numbers Explainer</a>.
* <strong>The Shanghai Finish:</strong> An outshot hitting a single, double, and treble of the same numerical wedge. While technically possible on several numbers, the classic "Shanghai" refers to <strong>120</strong> (Single 20, Treble 20, Double 20). Explore its history in our <a href="/darts/shanghai-finish-in-darts/">Shanghai Finish Guide</a>.</p>
<h3>3. Match-Format &amp; Rules Terminology</h3>
<p>Professional tournaments employ different regulatory structures sanctioned by the Darts Regulation Authority:
* <strong>The Bust Rule:</strong> Crucial to game strategy. Hitting too many points, leaving 1, or hitting zero without a double voids your current visit entirely. See our <a href="/darts/bust-rule-in-darts/">Bust Rule in Darts Guide</a>.
* <strong>Legs vs Sets:</strong> In standard ranking tournaments (such as the World Matchplay or Premier League), matches are played in straightforward legs. At the World Darts Championship at Alexandra Palace, matches are contested in sets, creating high-drama momentum swings. Read our <a href="/darts/legs-vs-sets/">Legs vs Sets Format Guide</a>.
* <strong>Double-In Double-Out:</strong> The demanding requirement to begin and conclude a leg on a double ring, tested each October at the World Grand Prix in Leicester. Read our <a href="/darts/double-in-double-out/">Double-In Double-Out Guide</a>.</p>
<h3>4. Slang, Equipment &amp; Fan Culture</h3>
<p>The sport's vibrant identity comes from its tavern heritage and lively arena atmosphere:
* <strong>The Robin Hood:</strong> When a player's dart sticks directly into the back of a previously thrown dart. While spectacular to watch, DRA rules dictate that points only count if the tip touches the bristle surface—making a Robin Hood score exactly zero. Read the rules in our <a href="/darts/robin-hood-in-darts/">Robin Hood in Darts Guide</a>.
* <strong>Tops vs Madhouse:</strong> Professional players design their outshot math to leave <strong>Double 20 ("Tops")</strong> or <strong>Double 16</strong>, because missing just inside leaves an even number for a clean follow-up double (Double 10 or Double 8). In contrast, <strong>Double 1 ("The Madhouse")</strong> leaves no margin for error.
* <strong>Bed &amp; Breakfast (26):</strong> Hitting single 20, single 1, and single 5 is the universal hallmark of pub darts, immortalized by generations of British tavern leagues.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What does "Madhouse" mean in darts?</h3>
            <p>In darts, <strong>"The Madhouse"</strong> is slang for <strong>Double 1</strong> (worth 2 points). It earned its notorious reputation because once a player is on 2, any single 1 or miss inside busts the score to zero, leaving them trapped in a mental and mathematical loop that is notoriously difficult to escape.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why is 170 called "The Big Fish"?</h3>
            <p>A checkout of <strong>170</strong> (Treble 20, Treble 20, Bullseye) is called <strong>"The Big Fish"</strong> because it represents the biggest, most prized catch possible in the sport. It is the absolute mathematical maximum finish in 501 darts and requires nerves of steel to land the final Bullseye.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Does a "Robin Hood" count for points in darts?</h3>
            <p><strong>No.</strong> Under official Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) rules, a dart must have its point touching the bristle surface of the dartboard to score. A dart stuck into the flight or shaft of another dart scores <strong>zero points</strong>, even if the first dart is in a treble or bullseye.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What are "Bogey Numbers" in darts?</h3>
            <p>Bogey numbers are scores of 169 or lower that cannot be checked out with three darts under standard 501 rules: <strong>169, 168, 166, 165, 163, 162, and 159</strong>. Regardless of where a player aims, there is no mathematical combination of two trebles and a double that equals these numbers.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why is the throw line called the "Oche"?</h3>
            <p>The word <strong>"Oche"</strong> (pronounced <em>ockey</em>) derives from early 20th-century English pub dialect. While an urban legend claims it was named after a brewery called "S. Hockey &amp; Sons", historical lexicography traces the term to the Old French word <em>oche</em>, meaning a notch, nick, or groove cut into the tavern floorboards to designate the legal throwing mark.</p>
          </div>
      </section>

